From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Craig Johnson <1545411+captainpacket@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 10:54:10 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] api: shard the vxlan create lock by target slot and host VNI
 tuple

MakeVxLan took ONE process-global mutex plus ONE global flock file for the
whole of observe -> create -> move-into-netns -> configure. That is correct
and far stronger than the invariant it protects, and it is the last global
serialization point left on the vxlan path after 0006 sharded the veth one.

What the critical section actually owns is two things, and only two:

  * the TARGET SLOT (netns, ifname). ensureExistingVxLan() reads it,
    SetVethLink() writes it and removeLinkInNSIfExists() deletes from it, so
    every actor programming that exact slot must be serialized.
  * the HOST VNI TUPLE (vni, lower device, UDP port). Linux refuses a second
    host VXLAN with that tuple, and isConflictingHostVxLan() matches
    candidates on exactly it, so the EEXIST -> clean -> retry dance is only
    ever contended between creators that share it. Note the remote VTEP is
    deliberately NOT part of the key: two wires to different peers with the
    same VNI on the same parent DO collide.

Nothing else in MakeVxLan is shared. The staging name is crypto-random
(0002), so temp-name collisions are not a concern, and the conflict scan is
already scoped to the target netns (0008) and only ever deletes a link whose
IFLA_IFALIAS carries OUR owner key (0004), so a creator with a different VNI
cannot be touched by another's cleanup. Sharding by those two keys therefore
narrows the lock to the collision domain without widening any scope.

Both layers are kept, because koko runs in two processes at once (the meshnet
daemon and the CNI plugin binary kubelet execs): a refcounted in-process
mutex per key, and an flock(2) on a per-key file. Keys are acquired in sorted
order, which is the deadlock argument; MakeVxLan is never called while a veth
endpoint lock is held, so there is no lock-order inversion with 0006.

KOKO_VXLAN_LOCK keeps working and keeps selecting WHERE the lock files live:
vxlanLockPaths() is untouched (its two existing tests still pass unchanged,
which is the evidence the path contract did not move) and vxlanLockDirs()
takes the directory half of it.

Why upstream wants this: it is a pure concurrency win with no API change and
no behaviour change for a single creator, and it is the exact companion to
0006 -- the same argument, applied to the other half of koko's wire surface.

Positive control: TestVxlanCreateLockSerializesOverlapOnly holds one wire's
locks and proves a disjoint creator proceeds while a creator sharing EITHER
the target slot or the host tuple blocks, then acquires on release. Executed
negative control: forcing vxlanCreateLockKeys() to return a single constant
key reproduces the old global lock and the test fails with "a disjoint vxlan
creator was blocked: the lock is still global".
---
 api/koko_api.go             |  77 ++++++++++++------
 api/koko_shard_lock.go      | 136 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 api/koko_vxlan_lock_test.go | 151 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 342 insertions(+), 22 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 api/koko_shard_lock.go
 create mode 100644 api/koko_vxlan_lock_test.go

diff --git a/api/koko_api.go b/api/koko_api.go
index 77473d3..8cc5faf 100644
--- a/api/koko_api.go
+++ b/api/koko_api.go
@@ -11,8 +11,9 @@ import (
